-- rules/profile.rofl — WHAT THE STORE'S SHAPE MEANS. Opt-in.
--
-- Reads scanners/profile_shape.ts's four relations and says which columns are
-- one fact about a WHOLE repeated once per PART, which are keys nobody
-- declared, and which are foreign keys that hold.
--
-- OPT-IN FROM BIRTH, AND THAT IS A PAID-FOR LESSON RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.
-- A profile reads every fact, which is the exact shape of `forged[audit]` —
-- leading with an unbound premise over a per-fact relation — and that shape
-- cost one hundred per cent of the evaluation of an AST index until it was
-- moved out of the kernel on 2026-09-09
-- (`f_four_rules_that_never_fire_are_the_entire_cost_of_an_index`). The
-- sequence is known; there is no reason to walk it twice. So this is a pack a
-- world loads when it wants to be profiled.
--
-- THE ENUMERATION IS THE HOST'S AND THE JUDGEMENT IS HERE. "No two rows
-- disagree" cannot be said positively — a rule would have to enumerate the
-- agreements — so what crosses the boundary is the WITNESS OF VIOLATION,
-- `disagrees`, and the rules below say `not disagrees`. That is the only shape
-- in which "none" is checkable by anyone who did not run the scan.
--
--   node --experimental-strip-types scanners/profile_shape.ts <files>
--        > facts/profile-shape.rofl

edb(grouped).
edb(disagrees).
edb(unique_col).
edb(included).
edb(census).

-- ------------------------------------------------------- an unfinished world

-- A PROFILE OF A WORLD THAT DID NOT FINISH IS NOT A PROFILE.
--
-- Every finding below rests on `not disagrees` — on nobody having contradicted
-- the dependency. In a world that hit a wall, the contradicting row may simply
-- NOT HAVE BEEN DERIVED YET, so silence means `not reached` rather than `not
-- there`. The discovered/declared gap is bad enough on a complete corpus; on a
-- partial one the analysis would report the shape of an interruption.
--
-- The kernel already says so: a wall emits a `hole`, which is this system's
-- word for `this is not an answer`. So the profile reads it and withdraws,
-- rather than answering a question it cannot answer. Reported and not merely
-- absent, because an empty profile and a refused one are the same two
-- characters.
unfinished[profile](W, R) :- hole(W, R).

-- Zero-arity is not in this language (`complete.` does not parse, and both
-- engines refuse it identically), so the subject is named. It reads better
-- anyway: the claim is about THIS world and one day there may be another.
sound[profile](this_world)         :- not unfinished_any[profile](this_world).
unfinished_any[profile](this_world) :- unfinished[profile](_, _).

-- ------------------------------------------------- the functional dependency

-- A column is a function of the group its row belongs to. Both premises are
-- needed and the first is the one that is easy to forget: without `grouped`,
-- a relation holding ONE row per group satisfies every dependency vacuously.
-- Measured: `ast_file` has exactly one row per file and reports as collapsible
-- beside the real finding, meaning nothing.
fd_holds[profile](R, P) :- grouped[profile](R, P),
                           not disagrees[profile](R, P),
                           sound[profile](this_world).

-- ------------------------------------------------------------- the two tiers

-- A MIGRATION: the column could become a fact about the whole, but no such
-- fact exists yet, so something has to be written.
collapsible[profile](R, P) :- fd_holds[profile](R, P),
                              not already_whole[profile](R, P).

-- A DELETION: the fact about the whole is ALREADY in the vocabulary and the
-- column repeats it. `ast_node(Id, Kind, File, Line)` against
-- `ast_file(RootId, File)` is this case — the schema is normalised and the
-- scanner denormalises it on emit, 14 678 times over 16 files.
--
-- Recognised by INCLUSION rather than by name: the column's values are exactly
-- the values of a column in a relation with one row per group. A list of
-- relation names here would be a second thing to keep true.
already_whole[profile](R, P) :- fd_holds[profile](R, P),
                                included[profile](R, P, W, Q),
                                one_per_group[profile](W).

redundant[profile](R, P, W, Q) :- fd_holds[profile](R, P),
                                  included[profile](R, P, W, Q),
                                  one_per_group[profile](W).

-- A relation with one row per group: it has a column that identifies its row
-- AND no column of it ever groups. That is what "a fact about the whole" IS,
-- said by shape rather than by name.
one_per_group[profile](W) :- unique_col[profile](W, _), not grouped_any[profile](W).
grouped_any[profile](W)   :- grouped[profile](W, _).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- the others

-- A key nobody declared. `ast_child(Parent, Field, Index, Child)` turns out to
-- be keyed by its LAST argument: every node has at most one parent, so the
-- relation is a function from child to (parent, field, position). Nothing in
-- the vocabulary said so.
undeclared_key[profile](R, P) :- unique_col[profile](R, P), P != 0,
                                 sound[profile](this_world).

-- A foreign key that HOLDS. Reported rather than required: a model may
-- legitimately name something it does not hold, and the moment this stops
-- being reported is the moment to look.
foreign_key[profile](R, P, W, Q) :- included[profile](R, P, W, Q),
                                    unique_col[profile](W, Q),
                                    R != W,
                                    sound[profile](this_world).

-- --------------------------------------------------------------- the trap

-- WHAT A COUNT OF DISTINCT VALUES WOULD HAVE TOLD YOU, and it is wrong about
-- half of them. A column of few distinct values is either a whole-level fact
-- repeated or a small alphabet, and only the grouping tells them apart. This
-- names the ones a census would have flagged and the grouping clears, so the
-- disagreement between the two instruments is itself queryable.
looks_redundant[profile](R, P) :- census[profile](R, P, Rows, Distinct),
                                  Distinct * 8 < Rows.

census_would_mislead[profile](R, P) :- looks_redundant[profile](R, P),
                                       disagrees[profile](R, P).
